orts data-parallel circuits. On a technical level, Sparrow introduces\na new sumcheck protocol for products of multilinear polynomials (different from the standard one), and\ndesigns a read-only streaming PIOP for it that achieves the above efficiency.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDeepProve: ZK proofs for LLM inference | Hysen Labs (https://hysenlabs.com/projects/lagrange-labs-deep-prove)\nciteturn2search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: yesterday; Answers come from the project's GitHub data, last synced on October 25, 2026, and from our analysis. ... Repository  | 2026-09-16  | 2026-10-25 ... A sumcheck and GKR approach proves the layered computation more directly, which is where the claimed 10 to 30 times speedup over the previous published state of the art comes from. ... It points readers to zkml/README.md for installation, model setup, the GPU build and the full end-to-end bench-llm tutorial.\n\nLagrange-Labs/deep-prove\n\n# DeepProve: proving LLM inference with sumchecks and logup GKR\n\nFramework to prove inference of ML models blazingly fast\n\n3,351 stars 104 forks Rust NOASSERTION\n\nGitHub Official website\n\n## At a glance\n\nWhat is it?\n    DeepProve is a Rust workspace that generates zero-knowledge proofs for full LLM forward passes. The README claims 10 to 30 times faster proving than circuit-based systems, but the install steps live in a subdirectory and the licence is not a standard one.\n\nWho is it for?\n    Adopt DeepProve if you need cryptographic proof that a specific model produced a specific output and you can accept minutes of proving time on a large CPU machine. Do not adopt it if you need sub-second proofs, if you cannot read the Lagrange License, or if your model is not GPT-2, Gemma 3, Llama 2, an MLP or a CNN.\n\nCan I use it commercially?\n    Check first. The repository uses a licence we do not classify automatically, so read its LICENSE file before any commercial use.\n\nIs it still maintained?\n    Yes. The repository last received commits 150 days ago.\n\nWhat is it written in?\n    Mainly Rust, according to GitHub's language statistics.\n\nAnswers come from the project's GitHub data, last synced on October 25, 2026, and from our analysis. They are not legal advice.\n\n## Star history\n\n  * Lagrange-Labs/deep-prove 3,351 ★ · -3\nStar history\nRepository  | 2026-09-16  | 2026-10-25\n--- | --- | ---\nLagrange-Labs/deep-prove  | 3.4k  | 3.4k\n\n-3 Stars in the last 39 days, measured from the daily GitHub snapshot. Compare with other projects\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPico 3.0: Real-Time Ethereum Proving with Just 4 GPUs (https://blog.brevis.network/2026/09/30/pico-3-0-real-time-ethereum-proving-on-4-gpus-with-100-bit-provable-security/)\nciteturn2search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: 2 days ago; The pipeline builds on a broad line of public proof-system research: the classical sumcheck protocol; GKR for efficiently verifying layered computations; LogUp and LogUp-GKR for efficient lookup arguments; improvements to ZeroCheck; WHIR for multilinear polynomial openings; and ideas developed in SWIRL. ... Lower peak GPU memory let us raise the chunk size from 2²² to 2²³ cycles, so each proof covers twice as much execution and recursion overhead drops. ... In the WHIR query path, better data reuse accelerated a core kernel by 6.89×.\n\n#### 100 Bits of Provable Security\n\nAlongside the hardware cut, Pico 3.0 reaches 100 bits of provable security, which means the security of every proof is something we can calculate precisely.\n\nWe get there by accounting for soundness round by round. Every interactive stage of the protocol carries a small chance of error, each of those counts against one shared security budget, and a proof is only as strong as its weakest round. In the current configuration, that’s the WHIR query phase.\n\nEvery bit of 3.0’s speed is earned inside that budget, so we can see exactly which future changes would cost security and which are pure engineering wins.\n\n#### Under the Hood\n\nFor a more complete technical picture, here’s what the new core is built from and how we engineered it.\n\nThe research foundations. The pipeline builds on a broad line of public proof-system research: the classical sumcheck protocol; GKR for efficiently verifying layered computations; LogUp and LogUp-GKR for efficient lookup arguments; improvements to ZeroCheck; WHIR for multilinear polynomial openings; and ideas developed in SWIRL. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nReleases · cloudius-systems/osv · GitHub (https://github.com/cloudius-systems/osv/releases)\nciteturn2search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 6 days ago;   * Release 0.52.0 \"Sparrow\" ...   * ZFS code extracted out of the kernel in form of a dynamically linked library `libsolaris.so`\n\n# Releases: cloudius-systems/osv\n\n## Release list\n\n  * Release 0.57.0 \"Magpie\"\n  * Release 0.56.0 \"Nightingale\"\n  * Release 0.55.0 \"Stork\"\n  * Release 0.54.0 \"Dove\"\n  * Release 0.53.0 \"Firebird\"\n  * Release 0.52.0 \"Sparrow\"\n  * Release 0.51.0 \"Phoenix\"\n\n## Release 0.57.0 \"Magpie\"\n\nRelease 0.57.0 \"Magpie\"\n\nLatest\n\nCompare\n\n# Choose a tag to compare\n\n## Sorry, something went wrong.\n\nFilter\n\nLoading [Input]\n\n## Sorry, something went wrong.\n\n### Uh oh!\n\nThere was an error while loading. Please reload this page.\n\n## No results found\n\nView all tags\n\nImage: @wkozaczuk wkozaczuk released this 20 Dec 12:20\n\nv0.57.0\n\n`e4854b2`\n\n# Highlights\n\nThis new release brings many new and exciting features and improvements including:\n\n  * New build mode to hide the non-glibc symbols and `libstdc++`\n  * ZFS code extracted out of the kernel in form of a dynamically linked library `libsolaris.so`\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nUnfaithful Claims: Breaking 6 zkVMs / deuterium's blog (https://deut-erium.github.io/2026/03/03/unfaithful-claims-breaking-6-zkvms.html)\nciteturn2search6 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; Crawled: last month;   * Lookup / LogUp: Prove “all my values appear in this table” by encoding membership as sums of fractions. ... Image: sumcheck_v2\n### The Sumcheck Protocol\n\nThe sumcheck protocol proves that a polynomial sums to a claimed value over the Boolean hypercube (all inputs in $\\{0,1\\}^n${0,1}n), i.e the claim:\n\n$$H = \\sum_{x_1 \\in \\{0,1\\}} \\sum_{x_2 \\in \\{0,1\\}} \\cdots \\sum_{x_n \\in \\{0,1\\}} g(x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n)$$H=x 1​∈{0,1}∑​x 2​∈{0,1}∑​⋯x n​∈{0,1}∑​g(x 1​,x 2​,…,x n​)\n\nThe naive approach would be for the verifier to compute all $2^n$2 n evaluations. This is exponentially expensive.\n\nThe sumcheck protocol is a clever interactive protocol that reduces the exponential number of polynomial evaluations to checking only one.\n\nImage: sumcheck_v2\n\nIn each round, the prover must send a polynomial $g_i(X)$g i​(X) such that $g_i(0) + g_i(1)$g i​(0)+g i​(1) equals the previous claim. If the prover is lying about the original sum $H$H, then they must lie about $g_i$g i​ somewhere. But since the verifier picks a random $r_i$r i​, with overwhelming probability, the prover won’t then be able to match the evaluation of the original polynomial.\n\n#### The Compression Trick\n\nFor degree-1 (multilinear) polynomials, $g_i(X) = a + bX$g i​(X)=a+b X has only two coefficients. Since the verifier knows $g_i(0) + g_i(1) = H_{i-1}$g i​(0)+g i​(1)=H i−1​ (the previous claim), we have:\n\n$$a + (a + b) = H_{i-1} \\implies b = H_{i-1} - 2a$$a+(a+b)=H i−1​⟹b=H i−1​−2 a\n\nSo the prover only sends $a = g_i(0)$a=g i​(0), and the verifier recovers $b$b. This saves 50% on communication costs.\n\nThe next claim in the chain is\n\n$$H_i = g_i(r_i) = a + b \\cdot r_i = a + (H_{i-1} - 2a) \\cdot r_i = a(1 - 2r_i) + H_{i-1} \\cdot r_i$$H i​=g i​(r i​)=a+b⋅r i​=a+(H i−1​−2 a)⋅r i​=a(1−2 r i​)+H i−1​⋅r i​\n\nThis is linear in $H_{i-1}$H i−1​! By induction, the final claim is linear in the original $H$H. If $H$H isn’t in the transcript, we can solve for it.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOraclizer Changelog - Oraclizer Docs (https://docs.oraclizer.io/changelog/)\nciteturn2search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; Crawled: last week; Released October 22, 2026. ...   * New entries for Derived Wiring, Sparse Merkle Tree (SMT) and Sumcheck Protocol.\n\n#### Added#\n\n  * New entries for Derived Wiring, Sparse Merkle Tree (SMT) and Sumcheck Protocol.\n\n## Glossary Correction#\n\nReleased October 18, 2026. Corrected the name of ERC-TRUST in the Glossary.\n\n#### Fixed#\n\n  * The ERC-TRUST entry now gives the standard's official name, Typed Regulatory Uniformity for Security Tokens.\n\n## StateSync-GKR Design and Evaluation Paper#\n\nReleased October 14, 2026. The StateSync-GKR: Design and Evaluation of a Reusable Sparse GKR Engine (opens in a new tab) paper is now available on arXiv.\n\n#### Changed#\n\n  * Added the paper link to Introduction, Glossary, System Architecture and FAQ.\n\n## StateSync-GKR Implementation and Licensing Corrections#\n\nReleased October 10, 2026. Updated seven documentation pages to match the published StateSync-GKR component and its current use conditions.\n\n#### Fixed#\n\n  * Clarified independent proof jobs, reusable circuit preparation and verification against the original request.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nzk-ML in 2026: A Perfect Proof of an Imperfect Model | HOGE Wire (https://hoge.gg/zkml-2026-perfect-proof-imperfect-model/)\nciteturn2search8 [wordlim: 200] Published: last week; Crawled: yesterday; Wyatt Benno of ICME Labs, which adapted a16z’s Jolt virtual machine into a zk-ML prover called Jolt Atlas, put the shift bluntly: traditional circuit approaches are expensive for functions like ReLU and softmax, but lookups eliminate the need for circuit representation entirely. ... The system leans on sumcheck and a lookup-based GKR protocol rather than hand-built circuits, and the company reports proof generation roughly 60 times faster and verification hundreds of times faster than the prior generation of tools. ... DeepProve  | Lagrange  | Sumcheck plus lookup GKR  | Proves real architectures over quantized tensors  | Open-sourced June 2026, 12M-plus proofs\nDeepProve ingests models in ONNX, safetensors, and GGUF formats, and GGUF is itself a quantized container. The breakthrough was never that zk-ML learned to prove floats. It was that the proven integer model got close enough to the float model, and fast enough to generate, that people stopped caring about the difference. The gap did not disappear. It became tolerable.\n\nFramework  | Maintainer  | Core approach  | How it handles fidelity  | 2026 status\n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nEZKL  | zkonduit  | ONNX compiled to Halo2 circuits  | int8/uint8 quantization, tunable fixed-point scale  | Production baseline; drives OpenGradient’s on-chain fee model\nDeepProve  | Lagrange  | Sumcheck plus lookup GKR  | Proves real architectures over quantized tensors  | Open-sourced June 2026, 12M-plus proofs\nzkPyTorch  | Polyhedra  | PyTorch/ONNX to circuits, Expander prover  | ZKP-friendly quantization  | Images fast, LLMs slow per token\nJolt Atlas  | ICME Labs / NovaNet  | Jolt zkVM adapted to ONNX tensor ops  | Lookups for exact non-linear ops over quantized values  | Research into product; on-device proving\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\ngnark package - github.com/consensys/gnark - Go Packages (https://pkg.go.dev/github.com/consensys/gnark)\nciteturn2search9 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 weeks ago; Crawled: 3 days ago; recursion/sumcheck Package sumcheck implements non-native sumcheck verifier. ... selector Package selector provides a lookup tabl